ermoor residents surveyed want the Ambermoor late-night licensing curfew scrapped.\nC10 (0.94) Water quality below the Ossel weir project has not worsened since it was built.\nC11 (0.69) Approvals under the Hollin Vale small-business licensing rule take an average of 450 weeks.\nC12 (0.94) Traffic from the Corriston Fields housing development on Furrow Road matched the planners\' estimate.\nC13 (0.45) Noise complaints in Ambermoor were unchanged under the Ambermoor late-night licensing curfew.\nC14 (0.94) The Ossel weir project produced at least the projected power in its first year.\nC15 (0.55) Venue closures in Ambermoor did not increase after the Ambermoor late-night licensing curfew.\nC16 (0.69) Fewer new shops opened in Hollin Vale in the year after the Hollin Vale small-business licensing rule than the year before.\nC17 (0.55) Cross-border theft in Doran dropped after the Doran-Arvel checkpoint opened.\nC18 (0.55) Late-night assaults in central Ambermoor fell by 15% after the Ambermoor late-night licensing curfew began.\nC19 (0.40) Residents of Hollin Vale wait longer for planning decisions since the merger of the Quillon and Hollin Vale councils.\nC20 (0.60) The Ierne Vale regional rail link came in on budget.\nC21 (0.69) Revenue from the Brisenholt services levy fell short of its projections by 30% last year.\nC22 (0.31) Inspections under the Hollin Vale small-business licensing rule found a drop in safety violations.\nC23 (0.31) All of the Brisenholt services levy was spent on the services it was raised for.\nC24 (0.94) Salmon runs on the Ossel did not drop after the Ossel weir project was finished.\nC25 (0.06) The Ossel weir project needed more maintenance spending than planned.\nC26 (0.40) The Ierne Vale regional rail link was approved over the objections of Dunmarrow\'s council.\nC27 (0.40) Council tax bills rose after the merger of the Quillon and Hollin Vale councils.\nC28 (0.06) The Corriston Fields housing development was built on land that floods re